    },
    {
      "id": "EsqQXuGn-UyQpNNtmnT-y",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1225.7853051119373,
      "y": -902.3620389236972,
      "width": 643.3809814453125,
      "height": 1200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#9c36b5",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "2PMapooio1SxNxo2GLmuA",
      "index": "b2Pl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 252204441,
      "version": 6772,
      "versionNonce": 1094313954,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790952355991,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "class Cell(Generic[T])\n__________________________________________________\n(description)\n\n# Is basically proxy of Grid for more low scale\nmanipulations and separation of scopes. \nCell is responsible for all of the operations on the cell\nlevel.\n\nIt also stored reference to grid and whenever.\nAny instance of cell, can be used to access another cell\nwithin the grid. like so: \n> cell(3,3).walls + cell(3,4).walls\nit also supports + operation with a tuple of ints,\nrepresenting the offset we want to move by within the grid.\n> cell(3,3) + Move.LEFT(=(0, -1)) -> Cell(3,2)\nWe can access attributes that are in T \ndirectly from the Cell itself\nAnd unpacking, like: x, y, _, _ = Cell(0, 3)\n--------------------------------------------------\n(attributes)\n\ny: int\nx: int\n_grid: Grid[T]\n_data: T\n--------------------------------------------------\n(methods)\n\n+ property(valid): bool\n    return True # can be redefined through inheritance\n# validates if grid and data are not None\n# if None, returns None\n+ def __new__(cls, y: int, x: int, data: T, ref: 'Grid[T]')\n+ __init__(self, y: int, x: int, data: T, ref: Grid[T])\n+ __call__(self, y: int, x: int) -> Cell[T]\n+ __iter__(self): Iterator[Any] (unpacking)\n+ __add__(self, other: tuple[int, int]) -> Cell[T]\n\n# for accessing attributes in T\n+ __getattribute__(self, name: str) -> Any\n+ neighbour(self, tuple[int, int]) -> Cell[T]\n..................................................\n# stuff we might not need in maze, but maybe good to keep\nsince it's abstract class\n\n..................................................\n-------------------------------------------------",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": "XyZHDd1NVjOgM_SoRDQV8",
      "originalText": "class Cell(Generic[T])\n__________________________________________________\n(description)\n\n# Is basically proxy of Grid for more low scale\nmanipulations and separation of scopes. \nCell is responsible for all of the operations on the cell level.\n\nIt also stored reference to grid and whenever.\nAny instance of cell, can be used to access another cell within the grid. like so: \n> cell(3,3).walls + cell(3,4).walls\nit also supports + operation with a tuple of ints, representing the offset we want to move by within the grid.\n> cell(3,3) + Move.LEFT(=(0, -1)) -> Cell(3,2)\nWe can access attributes that are in T \ndirectly from the Cell itself\nAnd unpacking, like: x, y, _, _ = Cell(0, 3)\n--------------------------------------------------\n(attributes)\n\ny: int\nx: int\n_grid: Grid[T]\n_data: T\n--------------------------------------------------\n(methods)\n\n+ property(valid): bool\n    return True # can be redefined through inheritance\n# validates if grid and data are not None\n# if None, returns None\n+ def __new__(cls, y: int, x: int, data: T, ref: 'Grid[T]')\n+ __init__(self, y: int, x: int, data: T, ref: Grid[T])\n+ __call__(self, y: int, x: int) -> Cell[T]\n+ __iter__(self): Iterator[Any] (unpacking)\n+ __add__(self, other: tuple[int, int]) -> Cell[T]\n\n# for accessing attributes in T\n+ __getattribute__(self, name: str) -> Any\n+ neighbour(self, tuple[int, int]) -> Cell[T]\n..................................................\n# stuff we might not need in maze, but maybe good to keep since it's abstract class\n\n..................................................\n-------------------------------------------------",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
